  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Cooler, but still breezy tomorrow.\n\n- Smoke to worsen for the region as a whole due to more fires in\n  the local area and smoke production from Washington State. \n\n- Warming and drying trend through the week, no rain expected for\n  the upcoming week. \n\nA dry cold front that passed through the region last night will\ncause much cooler temperatures today. Breezy west winds will usher\na cooler air mass into the region through the day, helping to\noffset some of the daytime heating. \n\nThose winds will also bring smoke from Washington and Oregon into\nthe region. Yesterday was a very active day for wildland fire\nacross the Pacific Northwest, and satellite imagery showed at lot\nof smoke production. The dry cold front yesterday also caused some\nlocal wildland fires to expand, so now we have more smoke coming\nin from the west and more local smoke to add to it. Overall, the\nsmoke conditions are expected to worsen by this evening and for\nthe next couple of days at least. \n\nThe ridge over the western U.S. builds back into the region this\nweek, causing a warming and drying trend again. Earlier this\nsummer when that happened it brought moisture from the North\nAmerican Monsoon with it causing higher humidity and\nthunderstorms. That will not be the case this time, and as it\nwarms up it will get much drier again with no rain in sight for a\nweek.",
